Step 3: Look at the correct choice. Choice D states “A number greater than 1 that has no positive divisors other than 1 and itself.” That matches the definition in Step 1, so D is the correct answer.  

Why the mistake happened: You interpreted “divided evenly by any integer” as “has many divisors,” but a prime has the fewest possible divisors (just two). Remember that “any” in this context means “all,” which is not true for primes.  

Deeper reasoning: Prime numbers are the building blocks of the integers because every integer greater than 1 can be expressed as a product of primes (its prime factorization). This property would not hold if a prime could be divided by many other numbers.  

